In article <CKJGsy.5IE@dscomsa.desy.de> pawlak@zeubac.desy.de writes:
>
>Cursed bell on the other hand leads to what you have seen: characters with 
>thousands HP's and power points - and this without taking any risks (as undead 
>absolutely no threat to a powerful, properly equipped character) - it is just
>a question of spending a few days in front of your screen doing the same again
>and again...

   Well, in my case the hit point building was just an entertaining side
 affect of using the cursed bell. And you can take "risks" with it, though
 just about nothing poses a risk for an advanced character, in that you
 can create a situation so that the bell will also bring in high level
 monsters along with wraiths and ghosts. It's pretty easy to surround
 yourself with various dragons and cockatrices (er... whatever the
 plural of cockatrice is). The whole point of the bell
 exercise was to build experience points by killing tough monsters. And
 sitting in one spot doing it is still more fun than roaming up and 
 down the dungeon several times waiting for them to be generated.
 
   As far as big hit points totals, anytime you pass 150 and have the
 standard protection, you're pretty much invincible to anything other
 than eels, mind flayers, cockatrice(s) and overeating. 

  This brings up an interesting question: what's the best strategy to
 hit really high final scores? Just as with other people,
 my ascended characters always fall in the 3.9 - 4.1 million point
 range. 


